From: sbc-builder <root@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: radxa-zero-3e: reset Ethernet PHY via MDIO bus

The PHY reset GPIO is described on the PHY node, so Linux only drives it
after it has read the PHY ID over MDIO. If the bootloader left the PHY in
reset, the ID read fails and stmmac reports "cannot attach to PHY
(error: -ENODEV)". Giving the PHY ID in the compatible avoids that, but then
phylib asserts the reset again on every detach and the GMAC can come up
without its PHY clocks ("Failed to reset the dma").

Describe the reset on the MDIO bus instead: it is released once before the
bus is scanned, the PHY is detected by its real ID, and it stays out of
reset across open/close.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3e.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3e.dts
@@ -32,14 +32,20 @@
 };
 
 &mdio1 {
+	/*
+	 * PHY reset on the MDIO bus, not on the PHY node: the bus releases it
+	 * once before scanning, so the PHY ID can be read and the right driver
+	 * binds without relying on the bootloader, and the PHY is not put back
+	 * into reset on every attach/detach (which left the GMAC without its
+	 * clocks: "Failed to reset the dma").
+	 */
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio3 RK_PC0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+	reset-delay-us = <20000>;
+	reset-post-delay-us = <100000>;
+
 	rgmii_phy1: ethernet-phy@1 {
 		compatible = "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
 		reg = <1>;
-		pinctrl-names = "default";
-		pinctrl-0 = <&gmac1_rstn>;
-		reset-assert-us = <20000>;
-		reset-deassert-us = <50000>;
-		reset-gpios = <&gpio3 RK_PC0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 	};
 };
 
